{{TABLE1}}

Table 1 lists the register. Of the {{papers}} papers, {{p_reproduces}} reproduce, {{p_fail}} fail, {{p_not_proven}} are not proven,
{{p_not_tested}} was not tested and {{p_withdrawn}} was withdrawn. The register holds {{claims}} claim rows
({{claims_tested}} tested): {{c_reproduces}} reproduce, {{c_fail}} fail, {{c_not_proven}} not proven, {{c_not_tested}}
not tested, {{c_withdrawn}} withdrawn. {{c_ours}} of the {{claims}} rows are our own baseline comparisons rather than
paper claims ({{c_ours_repro}} of them reproduce, e.g. "the commanded joints beat the world model"); on the
{{c_paper}} paper claims alone, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail.

## 4. Three failure classes

We propose three classes for the {{fail_rows}} failed claim rows. **A, edge latency:** the method may work, but on
the edge GPU it misses the real-time budget the claim needs; an adaptive chunking method takes {{ex_a_ours}} per
decision against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}. **B, zero-shot judges:** a model used without training on this rig to
judge success, failure or progress is no better than a trivial signal; a learned reward model detects failures at
AUROC {{ex_b_ours}} where episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}. **C, latent planners beaten by joints:** a
learned latent used to plan, rank or track progress does worse than the commanded joints or a copy-last /
action-only baseline; a latent world model ranks the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, against a bar
of {{ex_c_bar}} and {{ex_c_base}} for an action-only baseline. The three examples are illustrative, nothing more.
Per-row labels wait: under the protocol committed before any reading, they count only once a blind second reader,
labelling the same {{fail_rows}} rows on the same form, agrees with the first pass at Cohen's κ ≥ {{kappa_bar}}. The
protocol and the form are in the repository. The second reading has not happened yet.

## 5. Limitations

One rig, one arm, one operator. A failure here is a rig finding, not a refutation of the paper's own setting. The
verdicts were labelled by one person, at rest, not always blind to the arm. Of the {{pr_rows}} gates,
{{pr_same_commit}} are same-commit and {{pr_no_bar}} have no bar: for {{pr_unprov}}, the order of bar and result
cannot be proven from this repository. The commit-order audit is mechanical, but its {{sc_to_bf}} upgrades rest on
matching a bar text, and the second-reader check of the classes — the only independent check in the study — has not
been run. Under the bootstrap, {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile. Only {{l1_repro}} gates were rerun cold. And the
papers themselves were chosen for relevance to one stack, not sampled, so the reproduction rate describes this
register, not the field.

## 6. Related work

A standardized real-world benchmark on the same arm reports a failure and recovery taxonomy for VLA policies
[@2606.08881]; a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. The vla-eval tool decouples
VLA inference from 14 simulation benchmarks through a WebSocket protocol and Docker isolation [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method
family empirically [@2610.18869]. These evaluate policies; we re-test the claims behind methods (judges, world
models, latency) against bars fixed before the run, and audit our own pre-registration. The closest statement of our
thesis [@2610.40026] scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, among them reproduction evidence and
deployment affordability; its reproduction-evidence score is five audit milestones (configuration mapping, task
records, task coverage, training records, seeds) and, in its words, not a probability that a result is correct. We
give one pass/fail per claim on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, with the bar committed before the run and the pre-registration
itself audited.

## 7. Conclusion

On a €{{rig_eur}} rig, {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} tested paper claims reproduce under bars fixed for this
hardware before the run. The scarce resource was not compute — ${{cost}} covered all of it — but order: bars before
results. Auditing our own history found {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates whose order we cannot prove, and both
numbers belong in the same paper. The register stays open. The next papers are already scheduled in the register
file, and every number here can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`).

